  "analyzed_vuln": {
    "function": "NflogLayer::parseNextLayer",
    "file": "PcapPlusPlus/Packet++/src/NflogLayer.cpp",
    "description": "`parseNextLayer()` trusts the NFULA_PAYLOAD TLV's declared `getTotalSize()` and computes `payloadLen = totalSize - 4` without first requiring the TLV length to be at least the 4-byte NFLOG TLV header. In this PoV, `NflogTlv::assign()` also skips leading zero bytes, so the bytes `00 03 00 09` are reinterpreted as a misaligned TLV with length 3 and type NFULA_PAYLOAD (9). Subtracting 4 from length 3 wraps `size_t` to a huge value. `IPv4Layer::isDataValid()` therefore believes at least 20 bytes are available and an IPv4Layer is created over a truncated value; `IPv4Layer::toString()` later reads `ipDst` beyond the RawPacket allocation in `getDstIPv4Address()`.",
    "conditions": "A pcap packet using DLT_NFLOG/LINKTYPE_NFLOG must parse as address family 2 (IPv4) and contain a malformed payload TLV that is accepted as NFULA_PAYLOAD but has a declared total length below 4. For this PoV, leading-zero skipping turns `00 03 00 09` into length 3/type 9, and the following value begins with `0x46`, satisfying IPv4 version 4 and IHL >= 5. Because the wrapped payload length bypasses IPv4's minimum-size validation while fewer than 20 bytes are actually available at that pointer, calling `Packet::toString()` reaches the out-of-bounds destination-address read."
  },
